disease. For chronic pain management, small consistent behaviours are usually more meaningful than short periods of extreme effort. Management often combines education, graded activity, rehabilitation, sleep support, psychological strategies, treatment of contributing disease, and carefully selected medicines when appropriate. Goals commonly include function and quality of life, not only elimination of pain. The aim is not perfection but a pattern that is sustainable and appropriate to the person's circumstances.\n\n## What changes the picture\n\nManagement often combines education, graded activity, rehabilitation, sleep support, psychological strategies, treatment of contributing disease, and carefully selected medicines when appropriate. Goals commonly include function and quality of life, not only elimination of pain.\n\nThe practical lesson is to look for patterns rather than isolated moments. Trends over time, changes from a person's usual baseline, functional impact, and the presence of other symptoms often matter more than a single measurement or one day of discomfort. Where formal testing exists, results also need to be interpreted using the correct reference range, method, and clinical context.\n\n## Evidence, uncertainty, and health information\n\nHealth evidence is strongest when claims are supported by appropriate study designs and reproduced across more than one setting. A result that is statistically associated with an outcome does not automatically prove that it caused the outcome, and a treatment that helps on average may not be suitable for every person. Screening tests can produce false positives and false negatives. Symptoms can have multiple causes. Guidelines can also differ because populations, resources, and trade-offs differ.\n\nReliable health communication therefore states what is known, what remains uncertain, and what decision the evidence is actually meant to inform. It should avoid miracle claims, absolute guarantees, and the idea that one food, supplement, test, or habit can replace the rest of healthcare.\n\n## When to involve a professional\n\nNew weakness, loss of bowel or bladder control, fever with severe pain, major trauma, unexplained weight loss, cancer-related concerns, or rapidly changing neurological symptoms require prompt medical assessment.\n\nFor non-urgent questions, a qualified clinician, pharmacist, dentist, dietitian, physiotherapist, psychologist, or other appropriate health professional can help interpret symptoms, tests, medicines, and risk in context. Emergency symptoms should be handled through local emergency services rather than through online guidance.\n\n## What to remember\n\nChronic pain management is best understood as part of a larger health system rather than as a single number or symptom. The most useful approach is to combine credible evidence, attention to change over time, realistic preventive habits, and professional assessment when risk or uncertainty is significant.\n\nThis Blogdel article is general health education. It is not a diagnosis, prescription, or substitute for care from a qualified health professional.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/health-fallback-1.png","featured_image_alt":"Chronic pain management: How everyday choices affect outcomes","word_count":612,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["health","evergreen","explainer","Chronic pain management"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"NIH","title":"National Institutes of Health","url":"https://www.nih.gov/","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"MedlinePlus","title":"MedlinePlus Health Topics","url":"https://medlineplus.gov/healthtopics.html","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"health-archive-2026-245","published_at":"2026-09-28 12:00:00+00","slug":"chronic-pain-management-when-professional-care-becomes-important-245","title":"Chronic pain management: When professional care becomes important","description":"Chronic pain management explained through when professional care becomes important, with practical context, evidence limits, risk factors, and clear guidance on when professional care matters.","body_markdown":"# Chronic pain management: When professional care becomes important\n\nChronic pain management is a broad health topic, and useful guidance starts by separating normal variation from patterns that may need assessment. This article focuses on when professional care becomes important while keeping the discussion educational, evidence-aware, and non-diagnostic.\n\n## The health system behind the topic\n\nChronic pain persists beyond expected tissue healing or recurs over long periods and can involve changes in nerves, the spinal cord, brain processing, sleep, mood, movement, and social function. Pain intensity does not always correspond directly to ongoing tissue damage.\n\nHealth is rarely controlled by one variable. Biological differences, age, environment, access to care, social conditions, medicines, coexisting illness, and behaviour can all alter what is typical or safe. That is why a general explanation should help a reader understand the system without pretending to diagnose an individual case.\n\n## When professional care becomes important\n\nSelf-care and general health information have limits. Professional care becomes important when symptoms are severe, persistent, progressive, unexplained, or are interfering with normal function. It is also important when a person is pregnant, very young, older and frail, immunocompromised, taking complex medicines, or living with conditions that change risk. New weakness, loss of bowel or bladder control, fever with severe pain, major trauma, unexplained weight loss, cancer-related concerns, or rapidly changing neurological symptoms require prompt medical assessment.\n\n## What changes the picture\n\nManagement often combines education, graded activity, rehabilitation, sleep support, psychological strategies, treatment of contributing disease, and carefully selected medicines when appropriate. Goals commonly include function and quality of life, not only elimination of pain.\n\nThe practical lesson is to look for patterns rather than isolated moments. Trends over time, changes from a person's usual baseline, functional impact, and the presence of other symptoms often matter more than a single measurement or one day of discomfort. Where formal testing exists, results also need to be interpreted using the correct reference range, method, and clinical context.\n\n## Evidence, uncertainty, and health information\n\nHealth evidence is strongest when claims are supported by appropriate study designs and reproduced across more than one setting. A result that is statistically associated with an outcome does not automatically prove that it caused the outcome, and a treatment that helps on average may not be suitable for every person. Screening tests can produce false positives and false negatives. Symptoms can have multiple causes. Guidelines can also differ because populations, resources, and trade-offs differ.\n\nReliable health communication therefore states what is known, what remains uncertain, and what decision the evidence is actually meant to inform. It should avoid miracle claims, absolute guarantees, and the idea that one food, supplement, test, or habit can replace the rest of healthcare.\n\n## When to involve a professional\n\nNew weakness, loss of bowel or bladder control, fever with severe pain, major trauma, unexplained weight loss, cancer-related concerns, or rapidly changing neurological symptoms require prompt medical assessment.\n\nFor non-urgent questions, a qualified clinician, pharmacist, dentist, dietitian, physiotherapist, psychologist, or other appropriate health professional can help interpret symptoms, tests, medicines, and risk in context. Emergency symptoms should be handled through local emergency services rather than through online guidance.\n\n## What to remember\n\nChronic pain management is best understood as part of a larger health system rather than as a single number or symptom. The most useful approach is to combine credible evidence, attention to change over time, realistic preventive habits, and professional assessment when risk or uncertainty is significant.\n\nThis Blogdel article is general health education. It is not a diagnosis, prescription, or substitute for care from a qualified health professional.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/health-fallback-2.png","featured_image_alt":"Chronic pain management: When professional care becomes important","word_count":598,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["health","evergreen","explainer","Chronic pain management"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"NIH","title":"National Institutes of Health","url":"https://www.nih.gov/","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"MedlinePlus","title":"MedlinePlus Health Topics","url":"https://medlineplus.gov/healthtopics.html","authority":"primary","position":2}]}]$blogdel$::jsonb) as p(
  external_id text,
  published_at timestamptz,
  slug text,
  title text,
  description text,
  body_markdown text,
  article_type text,
  language text,
  featured_image_url text,
  featured_image_alt text,
  word_count integer,
  reading_time_minutes integer,
  keywords text[],
  provider text,
  model text,
  image_source_type text,
  image_provider text,
  image_model text,
  refs jsonb
);

update public.source_items si
set status='processed',
    rejection_reason=null,
    instructions=coalesce(si.instructions,'{}'::jsonb) || jsonb_build_object(
      'required_author_provider','chatgpt',
      'required_author_model','gpt-5.6-sol',
      'benchmark_cohort','sol-historical-archive-v1',
      'forbid_blogdel_text_generation',true,
      'non_diagnostic_health',true
    )
from _blogdel_health_payload p
where si.external_id=p.external_id
  and si.category_id=(select id from public.categories where slug='health');

update public.articles a
set slug=p.slug,
    title=p.title,
    description=p.description,
    body_markdown=p.body_markdown,
    article_type=p.article_type::public.article_type_enum,
    language=p.language,
    status='published'::public.article_status_enum,
    featured_image_url=p.featured_image_url,
    featured_image_alt=p.featured_image_alt,
    word_count=p.word_count,
    reading_time_minutes=p.reading_time_minutes,
    keywords=p.keywords,
    provider='chatgpt',
    model='gpt-5.6-sol',
    is_demo=false,
    event_at=p.published_at,
    published_at=p.published_at,
    archived_at=null,
    generation_job_id=null,
    image_source_type=p.image_source_type,
    image_provider=p.image_provider,
    image_model=p.image_model,
    updated_at=now()
from _blogdel_health_payload p
join public.source_items si on si.external_id=p.external_id
where a.source_item_id=si.id;

insert into public.articles (
  source_item_id, category_id, author_id, generation_job_id,
  slug, title, description, body_markdown, article_type, language,
  status, featured_image_url, featured_image_alt, word_count,
  reading_time_minutes, keywords, provider, model, is_demo,
  event_at, published_at, image_source_type, image_provider, image_model
)
select
  si.id, si.category_id, au.id, null,
  p.slug, p.title, p.description, p.body_markdown,
  p.article_type::public.article_type_enum, p.language,
  'published'::public.article_status_enum,
  p.featured_image_url, p.featured_image_alt, p.word_count,
  p.reading_time_minutes, p.keywords,
  'chatgpt','gpt-5.6-sol',false,
  p.published_at,p.published_at,
  p.image_source_type,p.image_provider,p.image_model
from _blogdel_health_payload p
join public.source_items si on si.external_id=p.external_id
join lateral (
  select id from public.authors
  where category_id=si.category_id and is_active
  order by last_used_at asc nulls first
  limit 1
) au on true
where not exists (select 1 from public.articles a where a.source_item_id=si.id);

delete from public.article_references ar
using public.articles a, public.source_items si, _blogdel_health_payload p
where ar.article_id=a.id
  and a.source_item_id=si.id
  and si.external_id=p.external_id;

insert into public.article_references (
  article_id, source_item_id, provider, title, url, authority, position, retrieved_at
)
select
  a.id, si.id,
  ref->>'provider', ref->>'title', ref->>'url',
  nullif(ref->>'authority',''),
  coalesce((ref->>'position')::integer, ordinality::integer-1),
  now()
from _blogdel_health_payload p
join public.source_items si on si.external_id=p.external_id
join public.articles a on a.source_item_id=si.id
cross join lateral jsonb_array_elements(p.refs) with ordinality as r(ref,ordinality);

commit;
